## 2026-07-14 — issue #1529 (purrdf 0.6.0 bump)
### G4: value_vocab bare-CURIE enum vs sh:in object-form enum — VERIFIED NOT A REACHABLE DEFECT

Producer: `crates/pipeline/src/stages/value_vocab.rs`. `enrich_value_vocab_enums`
emits each open value vocabulary as a standalone bare-string enum `$def`
(`value_vocab.rs:254`: `json!({ "type": "string", "enum": v.members.clone() })`,
members = sorted BARE CURIEs from `derive_value_vocabs`/`compact_iri`,
`value_vocab.rs:145`) and REPOINTS every value-vocabulary-ranged property — which
purrdf compiles as a node-reference `$def` `{"type":"object","properties":{"@id":…}}`
(`is_node_reference`, `value_vocab.rs:193`) — at that bare-string enum
(`repoint_to_enum`, `value_vocab.rs:215`). So the enum'd field IS an object property
(a node reference), which was the exact configuration flagged as a potential bug.

Instance form actually observed (the DECIDING evidence). The closed-record instance
schema is the generated pydantic model, and it types a value-vocabulary-ranged field
as a BARE `StrEnum`, NOT an `{"@id":…}` node object:

  packages/python/gmeow_models/accessibility.py:18-25  AccessibilityFacetEnum(StrEnum) whose members are bare CURIEs (facetVisual = "gmeow:facetVisual", …)
  packages/python/gmeow_models/accessibility.py:62     assertionFacet: list[AccessibilityFacetEnum] | None   (VALUE-VOCAB object property → BARE StrEnum string)
  packages/python/gmeow_models/accessibility.py:64     assertionSubject: list[Entity] | None                 (NON-value-vocab object property → the Entity model, i.e. an {"@id":…} node)

The contrast on lines 62 vs 64 is the whole finding: a value-vocabulary-ranged
object property is DELIBERATELY projected as a bare CURIE string in the closed
record (the vocabulary IS the StrEnum), whereas an ordinary object property keeps
the `{"@id":…}` node form. The packed JSON Schema agrees term-for-term:
generated/schemas/gmeow.schema.json:111-121 (AccessibilityFacetEnum = bare-string
enum) is referenced by :39-51 (gmeow:assertionFacet → anyOf[$ref …Enum, array]),
while the non-value-vocab :65-107 (gmeow:assertionSubject) keeps the object
`{"@id":…}` node shape. A pydantic-produced closed record — `model_dump(by_alias=True)`
→ `{"gmeow:assertionFacet": ["gmeow:facetVisual"], "gmeow:assertionSubject": [{"@id": …}]}`
— validates CLEANLY against schema.json: the bare-string facet matches the bare
enum, the subject matches the node-reference `$def`. The bare enum matches its own
instance form exactly, so no closed-world validation can fail.

Why the two encodings differ in schema.json (and why it is not value_vocab's defect).
The sh:in object-form members (`{"@id": "math:twoSidedAlternative"}`,
gmeow.schema.json:49854-49903 on math:alternativeSidedness) are purrdf 0.6.0's RAW
SHACL-shape-compiler output; the value_vocab enums are injected by the pipeline
AFTER purrdf compilation, in the bare form that matches the pydantic StrEnum
contract. The bump's own branch reconciles the pydantic surface by UNWRAPPING the
object-form sh:in members back to bare StrEnum values (commit 1290b4e52; see
packages/python/gmeow_models/math.py:19-25 Math_PValueAlternativeSidednessEnum whose
members are bare CURIEs "math:twoSidedAlternative"). That unwrap proves the intended
closed-record instance form is BARE for BOTH families — so value_vocab's bare
encoding is the CONSISTENT one; it is purrdf's object-form sh:in members left
inside the JSON Schema (not value_vocab) that are the outlier vs the pydantic-authoritative
bare closed record. Changing value_vocab to emit object form would INTRODUCE the very
failure feared here: a bare, pydantic-produced closed record
(`["gmeow:facetVisual"]`) would then fail an `{"@id":…}` enum.

Reachability. No in-repo consumer feeds an object-form JSON-LD instance against a
value_vocab bare enum. `crates/validate/src/instance.rs` (`validate_instance`, the
`gmeow validate --schema` engine) is a generic user-supplied-instance validator, not
an instance projector; the only in-repo schema.json instance check
(crates/pipeline/src/stages/yaml_ld.rs `annotation_fragment_validates_against_json_schema`)
validates an `@annotation` fragment, never a value-vocab field. The closed-record
instance projector IS the pydantic model, whose value-vocab fields are bare by
construction. So an object-form-vs-bare-enum mismatch on a value_vocab field is
UNREACHABLE in the closed-record universe.

Pre-existing / out of scope. `value_vocab.rs` is UNCHANGED by this branch
(`git log main..HEAD -- crates/pipeline/src/stages/value_vocab.rs` is empty); the
bare encoding predates the bump. The bump only changed the sh:in path (purrdf 0.6.0
object-form members + the pydantic unwrap that answers it). Reconciling purrdf's raw
sh:in object-form inside the JSON Schema against the bare pydantic closed record is a
separate sh:in-path question, not a value_vocab correctness defect, and is out of
scope for a dependency-bump PR.

