## 2026-07-14 — logic-layer seam cleanups (four seams)

Honest boundaries on the Seam-3 compile-logic cache narrowing (user-approved
mechanism, disclosed limits — NOT omissions):
- The cache narrowing is a DENYLIST (authored base minus pure-documentation
  predicates never read by the compiler), not the originally-planned allowlist
  "reuse the readers' own selection (sound by construction)". The readers key on
  their vocabulary entirely inline via structural bnode-walks, so the allowlist
  would have meant a disproportionate, high-regression refactor of the core logic
  compiler. Soundness is therefore GUARD-ENFORCED (a non-vacuous byte-identity
  test proves all five readers produce identical output over the narrowed
  subgraph) rather than sound-by-construction. A future reader that starts
  consuming a currently-denylisted predicate would be caught by that guard in CI
  when first exercised, not prevented structurally — but the denylist is a
  subset-of-never-read and new reader vocab is included by default, so it can
  never silently go stale.
- The practical cache win is BOUNDED: because derive_validation_shapes reads most
  of the TBox (restriction/domain/range closures), a structural edit still
  correctly busts compile-logic's cache. Only documentation/prose edits
  (skos:definition@x-gmeow-english, labels, dcterms/prov metadata) stop busting
  it. "Not busting on a TBox edit" would be UNSOUND, so this bound is correct, not
  a shortfall — but it is narrower than the issue's "busting on any authored
  change" framing implied.

Nothing was scoped down or deferred: all four seams are fully implemented (Seam 2
duplicate deleted; Seam 4 affine cell dogfooded with graph/correspondence
byte-parity; Seam 1 counts single-owned with projection-report byte-parity; Seam 3
sound narrowing). make check green after regenerate + snapshot re-bless.

